e format metadata.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: float, source_format: str):\n        self.value = value\n        self.source_format = source_format\nclass InputParameter:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates an input value, detects its type, and provides parsing to a ParsedNumber.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, original_value):\n        self.original_value = original_value\n        self.type = self._determine_type(original_value)\n        self.numeric_value = None\n        self.decimal_separator = None\n\n    def _determine_type(self, value) -> str:\n        if isinstance(value, int):\n            return \"int\"\n        elif isinstance(value, float):\n            return \"float\"\n        elif isinstance(value, str):\n            return \"string\"\n        return \"unknown\" # Should not be reached based on problem constraints\n\n    def parse(self) -> ParsedNumber:\n        \"\"\"Parses the original_value into a ParsedNumber and sets numeric_value.\"\"\"\n        if self.type == \"int\":\n            self.numeric_value = float(self.original_value)\n            return ParsedNumber(self.numeric_value, 'int')\n        elif self.type == \"float\":\n            self.numeric_value = self.original_value\n            return ParsedNumber(self.numeric_value, 'float')\n        elif self.type == \"string\":\n            s_val = self.original_value\n            if ',' in s_val and '.' not in s_val:\n                self.decimal_separator = ','\n                parsed_float = float(s_val.replace(',', '.'))\n                self.numeric_value = parsed_float\n                return ParsedNumber(parsed_float, 'string:,')\n            else: # Assumes '.' is the separator or no separator for integers\n                self.decimal_separator = '.' if '.' in s_val else None\n                parsed_float = float(s_val)\n                self.numeric_value = parsed_float\n                return ParsedNumber(parsed_float, 'string:.')\n        # If type is \"unknown\", per instructions, do not raise exceptions.\n        # This path implies an unsupported input type which is outside current problem scope.\n        self.numeric_value = None \n        return ParsedNumber(0.0, 'unknown') # Placeholder for unsupported types\nclass ComparisonResult:\n    \"\"\"DTO representing the outcome of a comparison.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, winner_original_value, winner_type: str | None, are_equal: bool):\n        self.winner_original_value = winner_original_value\n        self.winner_type = winner_type\n        self.are_equal = are_equal\nclass ComparisonEngine:\n    \"\"\"Stateless service that compares two InputParameter instances and produces a ComparisonResult.\"\"\"\n    def compare(self, a_param: InputParameter, b_param: InputParameter) -> ComparisonResult:\n        \"\"\"Compares two InputParameter instances and produces a ComparisonResult.\"\"\"\n        a_param.parse()\n        b_param.parse()\n\n        if a_param.numeric_value == b_param.numeric_value:\n            return ComparisonResult(winner_original_value=None, winner_type=None, are_equal=True)\n        elif a_param.numeric_value > b_param.numeric_value:\n            return ComparisonResult(winner_original_value=a_param.original_value, winner_type=a_param.type, are_equal=False)\n        else:\n            return ComparisonResult(winner_original_value=b_param.original_value, winner_type=b_param.type, are_equal=False)\ndef compare_one(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Create a function that takes integers, floats, or strings representing\n    real numbers, and returns the larger variable in its given variable type.\n    Return None if the values are equal.\n    Note: If a real number is represented as a string, the floating point might be . or ,\n    \"\"\"\n    a_param = InputParameter(a)\n    b_param = InputParameter(b)\n    \n    engine = ComparisonEngine()\n    result = engine.compare(a_param, b_param)\n    \n    return result.winner_original_value", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/138": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/138", "solution": "def is_equal_to_sum_even(n):\n    \"\"\"Evaluate whether the given number n can be written as the sum of exactly 4 positive even numbers\n    Example\n    is_equal_to_sum_even(4) == False\n    is_equal_to_sum_even(6) == False\n    is_equal_to_sum_even(8) == True\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer.\")\n    return (n % 2 == 0) and (n >= 8)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/100": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/100", "solution": "from typing import List\nclass InputValidator:\n    \"\"\"Validates input 'n' to ensure it is a positive integer.\"\"\"\n    def isValid(self, n) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if n is an integer and n is greater than or equal to 1.\"\"\"\n        return isinstance(n, int) and n >= 1\n\n    def validate_or_raise(self, n) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Raises ValueError if n is not a positive integer.\"\"\"\n        if not self.isValid(n):\n            raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\nclass SequenceGenerator:\n    \"\"\"Generates the sequence of stone counts for each level.\"\"\"\n    def generate(self, n) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Produces a list of n numbers, starting at n and incrementing by 2.\"\"\"\n        return [n + 2 * i for i in range(n)]\nclass Level:\n    \"\"\"Represents a single level in the pile with its index and stone count.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, index: int, stones: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Initializes a Level with a 0-based index and its number of stones.\"\"\"\n        self.index = index\n        self.stones = stones\nclass Pile:\n    \"\"\"Aggregates the levels of stones, representing the entire pile.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, n: int, levels: List[Level]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Initializes the Pile with the total number of levels and a list of Level objects.\"\"\"\n        self.n = n\n        self.levels = levels\n\n    @classmethod\n    def create(cls, n: int) -> 'Pile':\n        \"\"\"Factory method to construct a Pile, using SequenceGenerator to determine stone counts.\"\"\"\n        generator = SequenceGenerator()\n        stone_counts = generator.generate(n)\n        levels = [Level(i, stone_counts[i]) for i in range(n)]\n        return cls(n, levels)\n\n    def to_list(self) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Converts the Pile's internal Level objects into a list of stone counts.\"\"\"\n        formatter = OutputFormatter()\n        return formatter.format(self.levels)\nclass OutputFormatter:\n    \"\"\"Converts various level representations into the final list of stone counts.\"\"\"\n    def format(self, levels) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Formats a collection of levels (Level instances or integers) into a list of integers.\"\"\"\n        if not levels:\n            return []\n        if hasattr(levels[0], 'stones'): # Check if the input is a list of Level objects\n            return [level.stones for level in levels]\n        else: # Assume it's already a list of integers\n            return list(levels)\ndef make_a_pile(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, you have to make a pile of n levels of stones.\n    The first level has n stones.\n    The number of stones in the next level is:\n        - the next odd number if n is odd.\n        - the next even number if n is even.\n    Return the number of stones in each level in a list, where element at index\n    i represents the number of stones in the level (i+1).\n    \"\"\"\n    validator = InputValidator()\n    validator.validate_or_raise(n)\n\n    pile = Pile.create(n)\n\n    formatter = OutputFormatter()\n    result_list = formatter.format(pile.levels)\n\n    return result_list", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/140": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/140", "solution": "def fix_spaces(text):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string text, replace all spaces in it with underscores, \n    and if a string has more than 2 consecutive spaces, \n    then replace all consecutive spaces with - \n    \"\"\"\n    def _scan_space_runs(text):\n        \"\"\"Scans text for contiguous space runs and returns their start index, length, and characters.\"\"\"\n        runs = []\n        i = 0\n        n = len(text)\n        while i < n:\n            if text[i] == ' ':\n                run_start = i\n                j = i\n                while j < n and text[j] == ' ':\n                    j += 1\n                run_length = j - run_start\n                run_chars = text[run_start:j]\n                runs.append((run_start, run_length, run_chars))\n                i = j\n            else:\n                i += 1\n        return runs\n\n    def _replace_run(run_length):\n        \"\"\"Returns the replacement string for a space run of a given length.\"\"\"\n        if run_length >= 3:\n            return \"-\"\n        elif run_length == 1 or run_length == 2:\n            return \"_\" * run_length\n        return \"\" \n\n    def _assemble_transformed(parts):\n        \"\"\"Concatenates ordered segments into the final output string.\"\"\"\n        return \"\".join(parts)\n\n    parts = []\n    last_idx = 0\n    space_runs = _scan_space_runs(text)\n\n    for run_start, run_length, _ in space_runs:\n        # Add the non-space segment before the current run\n        if last_idx < run_start:\n            parts.append(text[last_idx:run_start])\n        \n        # Add the replacement for the current space run\n        parts.append(_replace_run(run_length))\n        \n        # Update last_idx to the end of the current run\n        last_idx = run_start + run_length\n\n    # Add any remaining non-space segment after the last run (or if no runs)\n    if last_idx < len(text):\n        parts.append(text[last_idx:])\n\n    return _assemble_transformed(parts)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/141": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/141", "solution": "import typing\nALLOWED_EXTENSIONS: typing.Set[str] = {'txt', 'exe', 'dll'}\nclass ValidationResult:\n    \"\"\"Container for the validation verdict and optional reasons.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, verdict: str, reasons: typing.Optional[typing.List[str]] = None) -> None:\n        self.verdict = verdict\n        self.reasons = reasons if reasons is not None else []\nclass FileName:\n    \"\"\"Represents a file name, its parsed components, and character counts.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, raw: str) -> None:\n        self.raw = raw\n        self.base = \"\"\n        self.extension = \"\"\n        self.digitCount = 0\n        self.dotCount = 0\n\n    def parse(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Parses the raw file name to extract base, extension, and count dots.\"\"\"\n        parts = self.raw.split('.')\n        self.dotCount = len(parts) - 1\n        \n        if self.dotCount == 1:\n            self.base = parts[0]\n            self.extension = parts[1]\n        else:\n            self.base = \"\" # Ensure base is empty if not exactly one dot\n            self.extension = \"\" # Ensure extension is empty if not exactly one dot\n\n    def count_digits(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Counts the number of digit characters in the raw file name.\"\"\"\n        self.digitCount = sum(1 for char in self.raw if '0' <= char <= '9')\nclass Validator:\n    \"\"\"Performs validation checks on a FileName object.\"\"\"\n    def validateDigits(self, fileName: FileName) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the file name has no more than three digits.\"\"\"\n        return fileName.digitCount <= 3\n\n    def validateDotCount(self, fileName: FileName) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the file name contains exactly one dot.\"\"\"\n        return fileName.dotCount == 1\n\n    def validateBase(self, fileName: FileName) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the base name is non-empty and starts with a letter.\"\"\"\n        if not fileName.base:\n            return False\n        return fileName.base[0].isalpha()\n\n    def validateExtension(self, fileName: FileName) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the extension is one of the allowed types.\"\"\"\n        return fileName.extension in ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS\n\n    def validate(self, fileName: FileName) -> ValidationResult:\n        \"\"\"Orchestrates all validation checks and short-circuits on failure.\"\"\"\n        reasons = []\n\n        if not self.validateDigits(fileName):\n            reasons.append(\"Too many digits\")\n            return ValidationResult('No', reasons)\n        \n        if not self.validateDotCount(fileName):\n            reasons.append(\"Incorrect dot count\")\n            return ValidationResult('No', reasons)\n\n        if not self.validateBase(fileName):\n            reasons.append(\"Invalid base name\")\n            return ValidationResult('No', reasons)\n\n        if not self.validateExtension(fileName):\n            reasons.append(\"Invalid extension\")\n            return ValidationResult('No', reasons)\n\n        return ValidationResult('Yes', reasons)\ndef file_name_check(file_name):\n    \"\"\"Create a function which takes a string representing a file's name, and returns\n    'Yes' if the the file's name is valid, and returns 'No' otherwise.\n    A file's name is considered to be valid if and only if all the following conditions \n    are met:\n    - There should not be more than three digits ('0'-'9') in the file's name.\n    - The file's name contains exactly one dot '.'\n    - The substring before the dot should not be empty, and it starts with a letter from \n    the latin alphapet ('a'-'z' and 'A'-'Z').\n    - The substring after the dot should be one of these: ['txt', 'exe', 'dll']\n    Examples:\n    file_name_check(\"example.txt\") # => 'Yes'\n    file_name_check(\"1example.dll\") # => 'No' (the name should start with a latin alphapet letter)\n    \"\"\"\n    file_obj = FileName(file_name)\n    file_obj.parse()\n    file_obj.count_digits()\n\n    validator = Validator()\n    result = validator.validate(file_obj)\n\n    return result.verdict", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/144": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/144", "solution": "def simplify(x, n):\n    \"\"\"Parses two fraction strings, multiplies them, and returns True if the product is a whole number.\"\"\"\n\n    class Fraction:\n        \"\"\"Represents a fraction with a numerator and a denominator.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, numerator: int, denominator: int):\n            self.numerator = numerator\n            self.denominator = denominator\n\n        def toString(self) -> str:\n            return f\"{self.numerator}/{self.denominator}\"\n\n    class FractionParser:\n        \"\"\"Parses a fraction string into a Fraction object.\"\"\"\n        def parse(self, fractionString: str) -> Fraction:\n            parts = fractionString.split('/')\n            numerator = int(parts[0])\n            denominator = int(parts[1])\n            return Fraction(numerator, denominator)\n\n    class Product:\n        \"\"\"Represents the product of two fractions and checks if it's a whole number.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, numerator: int, denominator: int):\n            self.numerator = numerator\n            self.denominator = denominator\n\n        def isWhole(self) -> bool:\n            return self.numerator % self.denominator == 0\n\n    class SimplifierService:\n        \"\"\"Orchestrates the multiplication of fractions and determination of whole numbers.\"\"\"\n        def isWholeNumber(self, x: Fraction, n: Fraction) -> bool:\n            product = self._multiply(x, n)\n            return product.isWhole()\n\n        def _multiply(self, x: Fraction, n: Fraction) -> Product:\n            product_numerator = x.numerator * n.numerator\n            product_denominator = x.denominator * n.denominator\n            return Product(product_numerator, product_denominator)\n\n    fraction_parser = FractionParser()\n    fraction_x = fraction_parser.parse(x)\n    fraction_n = fraction_parser.parse(n)\n\n    simplifier_service = SimplifierService()\n    return simplifier_service.isWholeNumber(fraction_x, fraction_n)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/143": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/143", "solution": "import math\ndef words_in_sentence(sentence):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a string containing words from the original sentence whose lengths are prime numbers.\n    Preserves original word order and separates selected words by a single space.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime(n):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a given integer n is a prime number.\"\"\"\n        if n <= 1:\n            return False\n        # Check for divisibility from 2 up to the square root of n\n        for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    # Validate input sentence length (NFR-001)\n    if not (1 <= len(sentence) <= 100):\n        raise ValueError(\"sentence length out of allowed range 1..100\")\n\n    # Validate input sentence characters (NFR-002)\n    if not all(c.isalpha() or c.isspace() for c in sentence):\n        raise ValueError(\"sentence contains invalid characters; only alphabetic and spaces allowed\")\n\n    # Parse sentence into words: trim leading/trailing spaces and split on any whitespace (FR-001)\n    words = sentence.strip().split()\n\n    # Filter words whose lengths are prime numbers (FR-003, FR-004, FR-005)\n    selected_words = [word for word in words if is_prime(len(word))]\n\n    # Join selected words with a single space (FR-006, FR-007)\n    return \" \".join(selected_words)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/145": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "from typing import List\nclass DigitSumCalculator:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates digit-sum computation logic.\"\"\"\n    def compute(self, value: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Returns the sum of decimal digits of the absolute value of the given integer.\"\"\"\n        abs_value = abs(value)\n        return sum(int(digit) for digit in str(abs_value))\nclass IntegerItem:\n    \"\"\"Domain representation of an integer input with metadata.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int, original_index: int):\n        self.value = value\n        self.original_index = original_index\n        self.digit_sum = 0 # Initialized to 0, computed by computeDigitSum\n\n    def computeDigitSum(self):\n        \"\"\"Computes and stores the digit sum using DigitSumCalculator.\"\"\"\n        calculator = DigitSumCalculator()\n        self.digit_sum = calculator.compute(self.value)\n        return self.digit_sum\nclass Sorter:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates sorting logic for IntegerItem objects.\"\"\"\n    def sortByDigitSum(self, items: List['IntegerItem']) -> List['IntegerItem']:\n        \"\"\"Sorts a list of IntegerItem objects by digit sum, then by original index.\"\"\"\n        # Python's sorted() is stable. Sorting by a tuple (digit_sum, original_index)\n        # ensures the required tie-breaking behavior explicitly.\n        return sorted(items, key=lambda item: (item.digit_sum, item.original_index))\ndef order_by_points(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts the given list of integers in ascending order according to the sum of their digits.\n    If there are several items with similar sum of their digits, order them based on their original index.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return []\n\n    items = []\n    for idx, v in enumerate(nums):\n        item = IntegerItem(value=v, original_index=idx)\n        item.computeDigitSum()\n        items.append(item)\n\n    sorter = Sorter()\n    sorted_items = sorter.sortByDigitSum(items)\n\n    return [item.value for item in sorted_items]", "base_status": "fail", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [[[1, 11, -1, -11, -12]]], "plus_fail_tests": [[[-5, -12, 9, 15]]]}], "HumanEval/142": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/142", "solution": "def sum_squares(lst):\n    \"\"\"\"\n    This function will take a list of integers. For all entries in the list, the function shall square the integer entry if its index is a \n    multiple of 3 and will cube the integer entry if its index is a multiple of 4 and not a multiple of 3. The function will not \n    change the entries in the list whose indexes are not a multiple of 3 or 4. The function shall then return the sum of all entries. \n    \n    Examples:\n    For lst = [1,2,3] the output should be 6\n    For lst = []  the output should be 0\n    For lst = [-1,-5,2,-1,-5]  the output should be -126\n    \"\"\"\n\n    class IndexClassifier:\n        def isMultipleOf(self, index: int, n: int) -> bool:\n            return index % n == 0\n\n        def classify(self, index: int) -> str:\n            if self.isMultipleOf(index, 3):\n                return \"multiple_of_3\"\n            elif self.isMultipleOf(index, 4) and not self.isMultipleOf(index, 3):\n                return \"multiple_of_4_not_3\"\n            else:\n                return \"neither\"\n\n    class Transformer:\n        def square(self, x: int) -> int:\n            return x * x\n\n        def cube(self, x: int) -> int:\n            return x * x * x\n\n        def applyRule(self, element) -> None: # element will be an IntegerElement instance\n            classification = element.classifyIndex()\n            if classification == \"multiple_of_3\":\n                element.transformedValue = self.square(element.originalValue)\n            elif classification == \"multiple_of_4_not_3\":\n                element.transformedValue = self.cube(element.originalValue)\n            else:\n                element.transformedValue = element.originalValue\n\n    class IntegerElement:\n        def __init__(self, index: int, originalValue: int):\n            self.index = index\n            self.originalValue = originalValue\n            self.transformedValue = None # Will be set by transform()\n\n        def classifyIndex(self) -> str:\n            # Delegates to a shared IndexClassifier instance\n            return _classifier.classify(self.index)\n\n        def transform(self) -> None:\n            # Delegates to a shared Transformer instance to set transformedValue\n            _transformer.applyRule(self)\n\n    class ListInput:\n        def __init__(self, elements: list):\n            self.elements = elements\n\n        def length(self) -> int:\n            return len(self.elements)\n\n        def isEmpty(self) -> bool:\n            return self.length() == 0\n\n    class SummationResult:\n        def compute(self, list_input: ListInput) -> int:\n            if list_input.isEmpty():\n                return 0\n            total = 0\n            for element in list_input.elements:\n                total += element.transformedValue\n            return total\n\n    # Global instances for utilities/services within this function's scope\n    _classifier = IndexClassifier()\n    _transformer = Transformer()\n\n    # Orchestration logic\n    elements = [IntegerElement(index=i, originalValue=val) for i, val in enumerate(lst)]\n    list_input = ListInput(elements=elements)\n\n    if list_input.isEmpty():\n        return 0\n\n    for elem in list_input.elements:\n        elem.transform() # This internally calls classifyIndex and then uses _transformer\n\n    result_calculator = SummationResult()\n    total = result_calculator.compute(list_input)\n    return total", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/15": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/15", "solution": "def string_sequence(n: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Return a string containing space-delimited numbers starting from 0 upto n inclusive.\n    >>> string_sequence(0)\n    '0'\n    >>> string_sequence(5)\n    '0 1 2 3 4 5'\n    \"\"\"\n    # Generate the sequence of numbers from 0 to n.\n    numbers = range(n + 1)\n    # Convert each number to a string and join them with a single space.\n    return ' '.join(str(num) for num in numbers)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/148": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/148", "solution": "def bf(planet1, planet2):\n    '''\n    There are eight planets in our solar system: the closerst to the Sun \n    is Mercury, the next one is Venus, then Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, \n    Uranus, Neptune.\n    Write a function that takes two planet names as strings planet1 and planet2. \n    The function should return a tuple containing all planets whose orbits are \n    located between the orbit of planet1 and the orbit of planet2, sorted by \n    the proximity to the sun. \n    The function should return an empty tuple if planet1 or planet2\n    are not correct planet names. \n    Examples\n    bf(\"Jupiter\", \"Neptune\") ==> (\"Saturn\", \"Uranus\")\n    bf(\"Earth\", \"Mercury\") ==> (\"Venus\")\n    bf(\"Mercury\", \"Uranus\") ==> (\"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\", \"Jupiter\", \"Saturn\")\n    '''\n    \n    # Canonical list of planets sorted by proximity to the Sun\n    PLANETS = [\n        \"Mercury\", \"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\", \n        \"Jupiter\", \"Saturn\", \"Uranus\", \"Neptune\"\n    ]\n    \n    # Create a mapping from planet name to its index for quick lookup\n    planet_to_index = {name: i for i, name in enumerate(PLANETS)}\n    \n    # Get the indices of planet1 and planet2\n    # If a planet name is not found, .get() returns -1\n    idx1 = planet_to_index.get(planet1, -1)\n    idx2 = planet_to_index.get(planet2, -1)\n    \n    # If either planet name is invalid, return an empty tuple\n    if idx1 == -1 or idx2 == -1:\n        return ()\n        \n    # Determine the lower and higher index to handle input order\n    lo = min(idx1, idx2)\n    hi = max(idx1, idx2)\n    \n    # If there are no planets strictly between the two (e.g., adjacent or identical),\n    # then hi - lo <= 1. Return an empty tuple in this case.\n    if hi - lo <= 1:\n        return ()\n        \n    # Slice the PLANETS list to get the planets between lo+1 and hi (exclusive of endpoints)\n    # The slice is already sorted by proximity to the Sun.\n    between_planets = PLANETS[lo + 1 : hi]\n    \n    # Return the result as a tuple\n    return tuple(between_planets)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/146": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/146", "solution": "def specialFilter(nums):\n    \"\"\"Write a function that takes an array of numbers as input and returns \n    the number of elements in the array that are greater than 10 and both \n    first and last digits of a number are odd (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n    For example:\n    specialFilter([15, -73, 14, -15]) => 1 \n    specialFilter([33, -2, -3, 45, 21, 109]) => 2\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    odd_digits = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}\n\n    for num in nums:\n        # Check if the number is strictly greater than 10.\n        if num <= 10:\n            continue\n\n        # Convert to absolute integer value for digit extraction.\n        abs_int_value = int(abs(num))\n\n        # Get the last digit by modulo 10.\n        last_digit = abs_int_value % 10\n\n        # Get the first digit by converting to string and taking the first char.\n        first_digit = int(str(abs_int_value)[0])\n\n        # Check if both first and last digits are odd.\n        if first_digit in odd_digits and last_digit in odd_digits:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/147": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/147", "solution": "def get_max_triples(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts triples (a[i], a[j], a[k]) from the sequence a where a[x] = x*x - x + 1,\n    such that i < j < k and a[i] + a[j] + a[k] is a multiple of 3.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 3:\n        return 0\n\n    # Determine the remainder of a[i] = i*i - i + 1 when divided by 3.\n    # i % 3 == 0  => a[i] % 3 = (0*(-1) + 1) % 3 = 1\n    # i % 3 == 1  => a[i] % 3 = (1*0 + 1) % 3 = 1\n    # i % 3 == 2  => a[i] % 3 = (2*1 + 1) % 3 = 0\n    # This means a[i] can only be 0 or 1 modulo 3. It is never 2 modulo 3.\n\n    # Count of elements a[i] that are 0 mod 3. This occurs when i % 3 == 2.\n    # The indices i are 2, 5, 8, ... up to n.\n    # This count can be calculated as max(0, (n - 2) // 3 + 1)\n    c0 = max(0, (n - 2) // 3 + 1)\n\n    # Count of elements a[i] that are 1 mod 3. This occurs when i % 3 == 0 or i % 3 == 1.\n    # This is simply n - c0.\n    c1 = n - c0\n\n    # Count of elements a[i] that are 2 mod 3. This is always 0.\n    c2 = 0\n\n    # A sum (a[i] + a[j] + a[k]) is a multiple of 3 if the sum of their remainders modulo 3 is a multiple of 3.\n    # Given c2 is 0, the only possible combinations of remainders (r1, r2, r3) that sum to a multiple of 3 are:\n    # 1. (0, 0, 0): All three elements are 0 mod 3.\n    # 2. (1, 1, 1): All three elements are 1 mod 3.\n\n    total_triples = 0\n\n    # Count combinations of 3 elements from c0 items (all 0 mod 3)\n    if c0 >= 3:\n        total_triples += c0 * (c0 - 1) * (c0 - 2) // 6\n\n    # Count combinations of 3 elements from c1 items (all 1 mod 3)\n    if c1 >= 3:\n        total_triples += c1 * (c1 - 1) * (c1 - 2) // 6\n\n    return total_triples", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/150": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/150", "solution": "import math\ndef x_or_y(n, x, y):\n    \"\"\"A simple program which should return the value of x if n is \n    a prime number and should return the value of y otherwise.\n\n    Examples:\n    for x_or_y(7, 34, 12) == 34\n    for x_or_y(15, 8, 5) == 5\n    \n    \"\"\"\n    def _is_prime(num):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime according to BR3/BR4.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(num, int):\n            return False\n        if num <= 1:\n            return False\n        if num <= 3:  # Covers 2 and 3\n            return True\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        \n        # Check for divisors from 3 up to sqrt(num)\n        # only odd numbers, as even numbers are already excluded.\n        for i in range(3, int(math.isqrt(num)) + 1, 2):\n            if num % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    if _is_prime(n):\n        return x\n    else:\n        return y", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/149": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/149", "solution": "def sorted_list_sum(lst):\n    \"\"\"Write a function that accepts a list of strings as a parameter,\n    deletes the strings that have odd lengths from it,\n    and returns the resulted list with a sorted order,\n    The list is always a list of strings and never an array of numbers,\n    and it may contain duplicates.\n    The order of the list should be ascending by length of each word, and you\n    should return the list sorted by that rule.\n    If two words have the same length, sort the list alphabetically.\n    The function should return a list of strings in sorted order.\n    You may assume that all words will have the same length.\n    For example:\n    assert list_sort([\"aa\", \"a\", \"aaa\"]) => [\"aa\"]\n    assert list_sort([\"ab\", \"a\", \"aaa\", \"cd\"]) => [\"ab\", \"cd\"]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Filter out strings with odd lengths.\n    filtered_lst = [s for s in lst if len(s) % 2 == 0]\n\n    # Sort the remaining strings by length ascending, then alphabetically ascending.\n    sorted_result = sorted(filtered_lst, key=lambda s: (len(s), s))\n\n    return sorted_result", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/152": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/152", "solution": "def compare(game,guess):\n    \"\"\"Determines how far off each guess was compared to the actual game score.\n    Returns a list of absolute differences, with 0 for correct guesses.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(game)):\n        if game[i] == guess[i]:\n            result.append(0)\n        else:\n            result.append(abs(game[i] - guess[i]))\n    return result", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/151": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/151", "solution": "from typing import List, Any, Iterator\nclass NumberElement:\n    def __init__(self, value: Any) -> None:\n        # Wraps a single numeric value.\n        self.value = value\n\n    def isInteger(self) -> bool:\n        # Returns True if the value is an int type or a float equivalent to an integer.\n        if isinstance(self.value, int):\n            return True\n        if isinstance(self.value, float):\n            return self.value.is_integer()\n        return False\n\n    def isNonNegative(self) -> bool:\n        # Returns True if the value is non-negative.\n        return self.value >= 0\n\n    def isOdd(self) -> bool:\n        # Returns True if the value is an integer and odd.\n        if not self.isInteger():\n            return False\n        return int(self.value) % 2 != 0\nclass InputList:\n    def __init__(self, items: list) -> None:\n        # Represents the caller-provided collection of numeric items.\n        self.items: List[NumberElement] = [NumberElement(item) for item in items]\n\n    def isEmpty(self) -> bool:\n        # Returns True if the list of items is empty.\n        return not self.items\n\n    def __iter__(self) -> Iterator[NumberElement]:\n        # Allows iteration over the contained NumberElement objects.\n        return iter(self.items)\nclass ResultCalculator:\n    def computeSumSquares(self, input: InputList) -> int:\n        # Encapsulates the compute-sum-of-squares use case.\n        if input.isEmpty():\n            return 0\n\n        running_sum: int = 0\n        for num_element in input:\n            if num_element.isInteger() and \\\n               num_element.isNonNegative() and \\\n               num_element.isOdd():\n                # Cast to int for squaring as value might be a float like 5.0.\n                val_as_int = int(num_element.value)\n                running_sum += val_as_int * val_as_int\n        \n        return running_sum\ndef double_the_difference(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return the sum of squares of the numbers\n    in the list that are odd. Ignore numbers that are negative or not integers.\n    '''\n    input_list = InputList(lst)\n    calculator = ResultCalculator()\n    result_sum = calculator.computeSumSquares(input=input_list)\n    return result_sum", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": [[[1.0, 3.5, -4.6]]]}], "HumanEval/154": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/154", "solution": "from dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import List, Optional\nclass RotationGenerator:\n    def generate(self, b: str) -> List[str]:\n        rotations = []\n        if not b:\n            return []\n        \n        n = len(b)\n        for i in range(n):\n            rotated_b = b[i:] + b[:i]\n            rotations.append(rotated_b)\n        return rotations\nclass PatternChecker:\n    def __init__(self, a: str) -> None:\n        self.a_input = StringInput(a)\n\n    def check_rotations(self, rotations: List[str]) -> MatchResult:\n        for rotation in rotations:\n            if rotation in self.a_input.value:\n                return MatchResult(True, rotation)\n        return MatchResult(False, None)\ndef cycpattern_check(a , b):\n    \"\"\"You are given 2 words. You need to return True if the second word or any of its rotations is a substring in the first word\n    cycpattern_check(\"abcd\",\"abd\") => False\n    cycpattern_check(\"hello\",\"ell\") => True\n    cycpattern_check(\"whassup\",\"psus\") => False\n    cycpattern_check(\"abab\",\"baa\") => True\n    cycpattern_check(\"efef\",\"eeff\") => False\n    cycpattern_check(\"himenss\",\"simen\") => True\n\n    \"\"\"\n    generator = RotationGenerator()\n    rotations = generator.generate(b)\n    \n    checker = PatternChecker(a)\n    result = checker.check_rotations(rotations)\n    \n    return result.matched", "base_status": "fail", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/155": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/155", "solution": "class IntegerNumber:\n    \"\"\"Represents an integer and provides absolute value and digit extraction.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int):\n        self.value = value\n\n    def abs_value(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Returns the absolute value of the integer.\"\"\"\n        return abs(self.value)\n\n    def digits(self) -> list['Digit']:\n        \"\"\"Extracts and returns a list of Digit objects for the absolute value.\"\"\"\n        abs_val = self.abs_value()\n        if abs_val == 0:\n            return [Digit(0, 0)]\n\n        digit_list = []\n        temp_val = abs_val\n        position = 0\n        while temp_val > 0:\n            digit_val = temp_val % 10\n            # Digits are appended in reverse order of significance\n            digit_list.append(Digit(digit_val, position))\n            temp_val //= 10\n            position += 1\n        # Reverse to get most-significant-to-least order\n        return digit_list[::-1]\nclass Digit:\n    \"\"\"Represents a single decimal digit and provides parity classification.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int, position: int):\n        self.value = value\n        self.position = position\n\n    def is_even(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the digit is even.\"\"\"\n        return self.value % 2 == 0\nclass Result:\n    \"\"\"Holds even and odd digit counts and provides tuple conversion.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, even_count: int, odd_count: int):\n        self.even_count = even_count\n        self.odd_count = odd_count\n\n    def to_tuple(self) -> tuple[int, int]:\n        \"\"\"Converts the counts to a tuple.\"\"\"\n        return (self.even_count, self.odd_count)\nclass EvenOddCounter:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates digit counting, handling validation and result composition.\"\"\"\n    def count(self, num: int) -> Result:\n        \"\"\"Counts even and odd digits in the given integer, raising TypeError for non-int input.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(num, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n\n        integer_number = IntegerNumber(num)\n        even_count = 0\n        odd_count = 0\n\n        for digit_obj in integer_number.digits():\n            if digit_obj.is_even():\n                even_count += 1\n            else:\n                odd_count += 1\n        \n        return Result(even_count, odd_count)\ndef even_odd_count(num):\n    \"\"\"Given an integer. return a tuple that has the number of even and odd digits respectively.\n\n     Example:\n        even_odd_count(-12) ==> (1, 1)\n        even_odd_count(123) ==> (1, 2)\n    \"\"\"\n    counter = EvenOddCounter()\n    result_obj = counter.count(num)\n    return result_obj.to_tuple()", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/158": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "def find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Write a function that accepts a list of strings.\n    The list contains different words. Return the word with maximum number\n    of unique characters. If multiple strings have maximum number of unique\n    characters, return the one which comes first in lexicographical order.\n\n    find_max([\"name\", \"of\", \"string\"]) == \"string\"\n    find_max([\"name\", \"enam\", \"game\"]) == \"enam\"\n    find_max([\"aaaaaaa\", \"bb\" ,\"cc\"]) == \"\"aaaaaaa\"\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def unique_char_count(word):\n        \"\"\"Calculates the number of unique characters in a string.\"\"\"\n        return len(set(word))\n\n    def _select_candidates_by_max(pairs):\n        \"\"\"Selects words with the maximum unique character count from a list of (word, count) pairs.\"\"\"\n        if not pairs:\n            return []\n        max_count = max(count for _, count in pairs)\n        candidates = [word for word, count in pairs if count == max_count]\n        return candidates\n\n    def _lexicographical_min(word_list_to_compare):\n        \"\"\"Returns the lexicographically smallest string from a list of strings.\"\"\"\n        return min(word_list_to_compare)\n\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n\n    word_counts = [(word, unique_char_count(word)) for word in words]\n    candidates = _select_candidates_by_max(word_counts)\n\n    if len(candidates) > 1:\n        return _lexicographical_min(candidates)\n    else:\n        return candidates[0]", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/157": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "DEFAULT_TOLERANCE = 1e-9\nclass Triangle:\n    \"\"\"Represents a triangle with three side lengths.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, a: float, b: float, c: float):\n        self.a = float(a)\n        self.b = float(b)\n        self.c = float(c)\nclass TriangleValidator:\n    \"\"\"Provides static methods to validate triangle properties.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def validateSides(triangle: Triangle) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if sides are positive and satisfy the triangle inequality.\"\"\"\n        if not (triangle.a > 0 and triangle.b > 0 and triangle.c > 0):\n            return False\n        return TriangleValidator.checkTriangleInequality(triangle)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def checkTriangleInequality(triangle: Triangle) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Verifies that the sum of any two sides is greater than the third side.\"\"\"\n        return (triangle.a + triangle.b > triangle.c and\n                triangle.a + triangle.c > triangle.b and\n                triangle.b + triangle.c > triangle.a)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def checkPythagorean(triangle: Triangle, tolerance: float) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the triangle satisfies the Pythagorean theorem within a given tolerance.\"\"\"\n        sides = sorted([triangle.a, triangle.b, triangle.c])\n        x, y, z = sides[0], sides[1], sides[2]\n        return abs(z*z - (x*x + y*y)) <= tolerance\ndef right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    A right-angled triangle is a triangle in which one angle is right angle or \n    90 degree.\n    Example:\n    right_angle_triangle(3, 4, 5) == True\n    right_angle_triangle(1, 2, 3) == False\n    '''\n    triangle = Triangle(a, b, c)\n\n    if not TriangleValidator.validateSides(triangle):\n        return False\n\n    return TriangleValidator.checkPythagorean(triangle, DEFAULT_TOLERANCE)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/156": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "def int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a positive integer to its lowercase Roman numeral equivalent.\n    Handles numbers from 1 to 1000, raising ValueError for invalid inputs.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(number, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid type: number must be int\")\n    if not (1 <= number <= 1000):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid range: number must be 1..1000\")\n\n    roman_map = [\n        (1000, 'M'), (900, 'CM'), (500, 'D'), (400, 'CD'),\n        (100, 'C'), (90, 'XC'), (50, 'L'), (40, 'XL'),\n        (10, 'X'), (9, 'IX'), (5, 'V'), (4, 'IV'),\n        (1, 'I')\n    ]\n\n    result_roman = []\n    temp_num = number\n\n    for value, symbol in roman_map:\n        while temp_num >= value:\n            result_roman.append(symbol)\n            temp_num -= value\n\n    return \"\